===Etymology 2===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|reprobare|reprobare, reprobatus}}. {{doublet|en|reprove}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛpɹəbeɪt/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-reprobate2.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To have strong disapproval of something; to [[reprove]]; to [[condemn]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=274|volume=II|passage=Lord Rotheles allowed it was a very sufficient cause for returning soon, and '''reprobated''' all delays of letters, though he confessed to being a very idle correspondent;...}}
# Of God: to [[abandon]] or [[reject]], to deny eternal bliss.
# To [[refuse]], set aside.

